WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.810
The scale typically used to justify a movie's
success is the amount of money it earns at

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.040
the box office, and that's understandable.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.540
If one movie earned more than another, that
means more people saw it.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.049
But, that definitely doesn’t mean it’s
better.

00:00:13.049 --> 00:00:17.410
Sometimes, truly terrific films flop hard
as far as the bean-counters are concerned.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.430
Here are some of the best box office bombs.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:21.910
Edge of Tomorrow

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:26.530
Without a proper marketing strategy that gets
butts in seats, movies can fail at the box

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:31.310
office — which is what happened to Doug
Liman's 2014 film Edge of Tomorrow, based

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.610
on Hiroshi Sakurazaka's novel All You Need
is Kill.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.940
The trailers looked like just another generic
sci-fi flick, except this time with a bit

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:40.270
of Groundhog Day mixed in.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:42.560
“What the hell?”

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.470
But Edge of Tomorrow is actually a whole lot
more than that.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:51.210
It’s a sci-fi film that channeled D-Day
into a future war against an alien race with

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.150
the ability to reset the day.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.470
That is until Tom Cruise comes along and breaks
their winning streak.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.200
It’s basically a video game brought to live
action, in the best way possible.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.570
Spawn, advance, die, repeat.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:05.980
Despite great reviews, Edge of Tomorrow was
a box office dud.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.850
And make no mistake, the bulk of the film's
failure hinged on its botched marketing campaign.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.110
The studio basically retitled it Live.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.110
Die.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:15.250
Repeat when it was released on video.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.890
If that doesn't scream bad marketing, nothing
does.

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:19.890
Dredd

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.140
Comic book movies are all the rage in Hollywood
these days, but that’s no guarantee for

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.140
success.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.470
Dredd from 2012 is the perfect example.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:33.710
Despite solid reviews from critics, it only
grossed $35.6 million against a $50 million

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:34.710
budget.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.760
The film takes a few liberties with the source
material, but there's no denying it captures

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.640
the attitude of the comics pretty well.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.400
It’s a brutal work of art, and the movie
that fans of the comics had waited decades

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.400
to see.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.580
Plus, it features a motorcycle chase scene
that rivals The Matrix Reloaded.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.310
That’s a pretty sweet bike, right?

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.380
Too bad it wasn't sweet enough to greenlight
a sequel...yet

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.760
"Negotiation's over."

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.240
Zodiac

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.110
The mystery of the Zodiac killer is a tale
that's arguably as intriguing and perplexing

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.740
as Jack the Ripper, which is why so many people
have tried to dramatize the story — including

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.290
David Fincher, who adapted Robert Graysmith's
crime book Zodiac for the big screen in 2007.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:18.030
Laying out an extensive, in-depth case like
the Zodiac killer for a general audience is

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.690
difficult, but Fincher crafts a compelling
retelling of not only the murders, but also

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.780
the manhunt surrounding them.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.420
"I'm not the Zodiac."

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.020
Couple that with a stellar cast that included
Robert Downey, Jr., Mark Ruffalo, and Jake

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.420
Gyllenhaal, and you've got a great drama.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:41.130
Unfortunately, the film grossed just $84.8
million at the box office, but found new life

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:45.270
on home video, scoring profits of around $20
million.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:46.270
Hugo

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.970
The director responsible for hard-R films
like Goodfellas, The Departed, and The Wolf

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.320
of Wall Street isn't exactly who you'd expect
to direct a PG movie, but Martin Scorsese

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.489
did a great job with the dramatic adventure
Hugo.

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:03.720
Hugo received stellar reviews from critics,
but that wasn’t enough to propel the movie

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.030
into box office glory.

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:13.080
It grossed almost $186 million worldwide against
an estimated production budget of $150 to

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:14.080
$170 million.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.320
Although it failed to reach the box office
heights, Hugo is still a brilliant film and

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.270
a rare look into the rise of automation in
the early 20th century.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:27.180
Scorsese blended steampunk elements into a
historical film with a Parisian backdrop — a

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.330
combination seldom seen in Hollywood, drawing
cues from A Trip to the Moon and The Impossible

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.100
Voyage rather than modern superhero flicks.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.180
And that's what makes it beautiful.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.180
The Road

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.431
Based on the novel of the same name by Cormac
McCarthy, The Road was released in 2009 and

00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:48.910
grossed a mere $27.6 million against a $25
million production budget.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.050
But the fact that the film made it out at
all is a miracle.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:56.709
Anyone who's read a McCarthy novel knows how
difficult they are to translate onto the silver

00:03:56.709 --> 00:03:57.360
screen.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.400
Seriously, it’s just a whole lot of that.

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:07.750
How do you show audiences that the film they're
watching is set in a post-apocalyptic world?

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.810
Simple: make everything dark and gray.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.240
There's rarely been a more haunting representation
of a post-apocalyptic world, and that's why

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.840
this film is so important to the genre.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.009
It isn't about why the world is the way it
is.

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:22.409
Instead, it’s about the father-son relationship
of the main characters.

00:04:22.409 --> 00:04:26.589
Sure, it might be bleak, but it’s a story
well worth seeing.

00:04:26.589 --> 00:04:29.639
Master and Commander: The Far Side of the
World

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:34.400
Based on Patrick O'Brian's naval novel, Master
and Commander was meant to be a war movie

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:35.490
blockbuster.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:39.759
Unfortunately for Russell Crowe, who starred
in the often forgotten film, it was released

00:04:39.759 --> 00:04:44.069
the same year as the cartoonish, non-war blockbuster,
Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of the

00:04:44.069 --> 00:04:47.779
Black Pearl, which introduced Johnny Depp's
Captain Jack Sparrow to the world.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:51.460
Turns out, there wasn’t enough room for
two adventures on the high seas.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.259
“I’m Captain jack Sparrow”

00:04:55.259 --> 00:04:56.449
But forget all that junk.

00:04:56.449 --> 00:05:01.349
Master and Commander is a feast for the eyes,
depicting an often forgotten period of European

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:04.210
history, and it does so remarkably well.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.879
Unlike many other historical dramas, it gets
the real-life details mostly right.

00:05:08.879 --> 00:05:12.009
And Crowe brings it all home with his exceptional
performance.

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:17.229
The fact that the film received a whopping
ten Oscar nominations underscores its excellence,

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:21.759
and legendary film critic Roger Ebert gave
the film a rare perfect score, saying "it

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:24.770
achieves the epic without losing sight of
the human.”

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.129
Speed Racer

00:05:26.129 --> 00:05:30.409
The Wachowskis made a name for themselves
by writing and directing The Matrix trilogy.

00:05:30.409 --> 00:05:34.439
When it came time for them to tackle a new
project, they chose to bring the classic Speed

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:36.939
Racer series to life on the big screen.

00:05:36.939 --> 00:05:40.949
With the Wachowskis at the helm and the name
recognition of the property, it was a shock

00:05:40.949 --> 00:05:47.689
to see Speed Racer flop at the box office,
grossing $93.9 million on a budget of $120

00:05:47.689 --> 00:05:48.880
million.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.140
"Whoa."

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.779
The film's cartoonish visuals may have turned
off some critics, but for franchise fans,

00:05:54.779 --> 00:05:58.710
it was exactly what the film needed to capture
the awe of the original anime.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:03.199
What's interesting, though, is that the movie
includes themes of politics and corruption,

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:08.370
as well as general issues in society, and
those themes unfold on a number of levels.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.919
Yet it never forgets that it's still a kids'
movie.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:15.069
Speed Racer didn't fall into the trend of
gritty re-imaginings that plagued the 2000s.

00:06:15.069 --> 00:06:20.439
Instead, it takes a funny, witty, and light-hearted
charm that’s exactly what fans wanted.

00:06:20.439 --> 00:06:23.020
It’s just too bad more people didn’t see
it.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:24.159
The BFG

00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:28.180
Steven Spielberg ranks among the greatest
living filmmakers, and the fact that he's

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:32.210
the highest-grossing director of all time
only bolsters his legacy.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.449
He's the guy that pretty much created the
summer blockbuster, after all.

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:41.509
But none of that helped The BFG gross more
than $183.2 million worldwide on an estimated

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:44.699
$140 million budget in 2016.

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:46.699
The movie was a recipe for success.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:51.240
It was directed by Spielberg, distributed
by Disney, and is based on the famed Roald

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.099
Dahl novel of the same name.

00:06:53.099 --> 00:06:56.699
So why it failed to break $200 million is
a mystery.

00:06:56.699 --> 00:07:01.029
Monetary problems aside, The BFG is a criminally
underrated film.

00:07:01.029 --> 00:07:05.819
Despite its big budget, fantastical elements,
and summer release, it was still far from

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:07.669
your traditional summer tentpole.

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:11.330
Basically, it was a big-budget fantasy, without
the extravaganza.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.719
It's not so much about the spectacle as it
is about the journey, and that's where audiences

00:07:15.719 --> 00:07:20.589
will find themselves captivated by Mark Rylance's
outstanding motion-capture performance as

00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:24.099
well as the irresistible charm of Hollywood
newcomer Ruby Barnhill.

00:07:24.099 --> 00:07:27.469
Do yourself a big favor and check out The
BFG, ASAP.

00:07:27.469 --> 00:07:29.180
Thanks for watching!

00:07:29.180 --> 00:07:32.029
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:32.029 --> 00:07:34.319
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love too!

